\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Maxwell visco-elastic two-regime behavior. Distances are normalised by the Maxwell length $\ell _M = a\tau _{R,\mathrm  {ref}}$, times by $\tau _{R,\mathrm  {ref}}$, velocities by $\omega a/\pi $, and displacements by $\omega $. Grey shading marks the elastic-rebound zone ($x < \ell _M$ in panel \textbf  {a}; $t < \tau _R$ in panel \textbf  {b}). \textbf  {(a)}~Velocity at $t = \tau _{R,\mathrm  {ref}}$ for four values of $\tau _R/\tau _{R,\mathrm  {ref}}$ (0, 0.2, 1, 5). The black curve is the purely viscous Lorentzian, peaking at $x = \ell _M$ (dashed line). Increasing $\tau _R$ suppresses near-field velocity for $x \lesssim \ell _M$; at $\tau _R/\tau _{R,\mathrm  {ref}} = 5$ (dashed red) the near-field amplitude is reduced by more than 60\%. \textbf  {(b)}~Cumulative displacement at $x = \ell _M$ for the viscous model (black) and two Maxwell cases ($\tau _R/\tau _{R,\mathrm  {ref}} = 1$, orange; $= 5$, dashed red). Orange shading marks the displacement deficit relative to viscous. Both Maxwell models converge to the common asymptote $\omega /2$ (dotted) after $t \gg \tau _R$.}}{16}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:maxwell_regimes}{{3}{16}{Maxwell visco-elastic two-regime behavior. Distances are normalised by the Maxwell length $\ell _M = a\tau _{R,\mathrm {ref}}$, times by $\tau _{R,\mathrm {ref}}$, velocities by $\omega a/\pi $, and displacements by $\omega $. Grey shading marks the elastic-rebound zone ($x < \ell _M$ in panel \textbf {a}; $t < \tau _R$ in panel \textbf {b}). \textbf {(a)}~Velocity at $t = \tau _{R,\mathrm {ref}}$ for four values of $\tau _R/\tau _{R,\mathrm {ref}}$ (0, 0.2, 1, 5). The black curve is the purely viscous Lorentzian, peaking at $x = \ell _M$ (dashed line). Increasing $\tau _R$ suppresses near-field velocity for $x \lesssim \ell _M$; at $\tau _R/\tau _{R,\mathrm {ref}} = 5$ (dashed red) the near-field amplitude is reduced by more than 60\%. \textbf {(b)}~Cumulative displacement at $x = \ell _M$ for the viscous model (black) and two Maxwell cases ($\tau _R/\tau _{R,\mathrm {ref}} = 1$, orange; $= 5$, dashed red). Orange shading marks the displacement deficit relative to viscous. Both Maxwell models converge to the common asymptote $\omega /2$ (dotted) after $t \gg \tau _R$}{figure.3}{}}
